          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"If the planet has natural satellites , its mass can be calculated using Newton's law of universal gravitation to derive a generalization of Kepler's third law that includes the mass of the planet and its moon.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p6\",\"quote\":\"Thus, if the object's size is known, a limit on the mass can be determined.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p8\",\"quote\":\"It is impossible to \\\"weigh\\\" a planet, and much less the Sun, against the sort of mass standards which are used in the laboratory. On the other hand, the orbits of the planets give a great range of observational data as to the relative positions of each body, and these positions can be compared to their relative masses using Newton's law of universal gravitation\"}]}"
